\title{Restriction Algebra, Overlap Gluing, and Quartet Transfer\\for Multiple Phylogenetic Trees}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 21, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We develop a structural framework for agreement subtrees in finite families of unrooted phylogenetic trees. A tree is represented by a finite system of chosen edge-split sides, and restriction to retained leaves is modeled by intersection. Restriction is functorial, agreement is hereditary, common agreement is equivalent to pairwise agreement, and local witnesses glue across overlapping families and finite overlap chains. A restriction to $a$ leaves has at most $2^a$ distinct split sides. We formulate agreement thresholds, establish their monotonicity and exact one-tree base case, and prove that every bound forcing a common agreement subtree of size $n\geq4$ also forces a common quartet without quantitative loss. Consequently, any fourfold iterated-exponential upper bound for the multiple-tree agreement problem transfers unchanged to the common-quartet threshold. The framework applies to arbitrary finite split systems; tree compatibility is needed only in the quantitative counting layer.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Several unrooted binary phylogenetic trees may be defined on the same labelled leaves while differing globally. The multiple-tree maximum agreement subtree problem asks how many leaves can be retained so that all induced trees coincide. Its smallest nontrivial case has four leaves. For labels $a,b,c,d$, the three resolved quartet types are
\[
ab\mid cd,\qquad ac\mid bd,\qquad ad\mid bc.
\]
A common-quartet threshold asks how large a common label set forces $k$ trees to agree on at least one four-label subset.

Deleting an edge of a tree partitions its leaves. By consistently recording one side of each edge split, one represents the tree by a finite family of subsets. Pruning to a retained set becomes intersection. This separates the problem into a universal structural layer and a compatibility-sensitive quantitative layer. The present paper establishes the structural layer: composition and heredity of restriction, pairwise characterizations, overlap gluing, universal information bounds, and lossless transfer from larger agreement subtrees to quartets.

\section{Split systems and restriction}

Let $X$ be a finite leaf set.

\begin{definition}[Finite split system]
A finite split system on $X$ is a finite family $T$ of subsets of $X$. In the phylogenetic interpretation, its members are consistently selected sides of edge-induced bipartitions.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Restriction]
For $A\subseteq X$, define
\[
\restr{T}{A}=\{s\cap A:s\in T\}.
\]
Duplicate intersections are identified.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Agreement and common agreement]
Systems $T$ and $U$ \emph{agree on $A$} when $\restr{T}{A}=\restr{U}{A}$. An indexed family $(T_i)_{i\in F}$ has a \emph{common agreement subtree on $A$} when some split system $R$ satisfies $\restr{T_i}{A}=R$ for every $i\in F$. The state $R$ is a common restriction witness.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Restriction Composition]
For every split system $T$ and leaf sets $A,B$,
\[
\restr{(\restr{T}{A})}{B}=\restr{T}{A\cap B}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Every member on the left is $(s\cap A)\cap B=s\cap(A\cap B)$ for some $s\in T$, and every member on the right arises this way.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Nested Restriction]
If $B\subseteq A$, then $\restr{(\restr{T}{A})}{B}=\restr{T}{B}$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Use $A\cap B=B$ in the preceding theorem.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Full and Empty Restrictions]
If every $s\in T$ satisfies $s\subseteq A$, then $\restr{T}{A}=T$. Moreover,
\[
\restr{T}{\varnothing}=\begin{cases}
\varnothing,&T=\varnothing,\\
\{\varnothing\},&T\neq\varnothing.
\end{cases}
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
In the first claim, $s\cap A=s$ for every side. In the second, every side intersects the empty set in $\varnothing$; no resulting side exists exactly when no input side exists.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Union Law]
For split systems $T,U$,
\[
\restr{(T\cup U)}{A}=\restr{T}{A}\cup\restr{U}{A}.
\]
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
A side in $T\cup U$ belongs to $T$ or $U$, and intersection with $A$ is the same operation in either case.
\end{proof}

\section{Agreement and heredity}

For fixed $A$, agreement is equality of restricted systems and is therefore reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.

\begin{theorem}[Pairwise Heredity]
If $T$ and $U$ agree on $A$ and $B\subseteq A$, then they agree on $B$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Restrict the equality $\restr{T}{A}=\restr{U}{A}$ to $B$ and apply nested restriction to both sides.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Common-Agreement Heredity]
If a finite family has a common agreement subtree on $A$ and $B\subseteq A$, then it has a common agreement subtree on $B$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $R$ witnesses agreement on $A$, restrict each equality $\restr{T_i}{A}=R$ to $B$. The system $\restr{R}{B}$ is a common witness on $B$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Base-Tree Characterization]
Let $F$ be nonempty. The family $(T_i)_{i\in F}$ has a common agreement subtree on $A$ if and only if there is a base index $b\in F$ such that every $T_i$ agrees with $T_b$ on $A$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A common witness makes every restriction equal to the base restriction. Conversely, if every restriction equals $\restr{T_b}{A}$, that state is a common witness.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Pairwise Characterization]
Let $F$ be nonempty. The family $(T_i)_{i\in F}$ has a common agreement subtree on $A$ if and only if every pair $i,j\in F$ agrees on $A$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A common witness implies pairwise equality. Conversely choose $b\in F$; pairwise agreement applied to $(i,b)$ for every $i$ gives the base-tree criterion.
\end{proof}

Thus common agreement can be tested using one base restriction and $|F|-1$ comparisons, rather than all $\binom{|F|}{2}$ pairwise comparisons.

\section{Overlap gluing}

\begin{theorem}[Two-Family Overlap Gluing]
Let $F\cap G\neq\varnothing$. If the systems indexed by $F$ have common restriction $R$ on $A$ and those indexed by $G$ have common restriction $S$ on $A$, then $R=S$ and all systems indexed by $F\cup G$ have a common restriction on $A$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $c\in F\cap G$. Then $\restr{T_c}{A}=R$ by membership in $F$ and $\restr{T_c}{A}=S$ by membership in $G$. Hence $R=S$, and this state witnesses agreement for the union.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cross-Family Agreement]
Under the preceding hypotheses, every $i\in F$ agrees on $A$ with every $j\in G$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Both restrictions equal the witness for the union.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Overlap Chain]
A finite list $F_1,\ldots,F_m$ is an overlap chain when $F_i\cap F_{i+1}\neq\varnothing$ for every $1\leq i<m$. Empty and singleton lists satisfy this condition vacuously.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Chain Gluing]
If $F_1,\ldots,F_m$ is an overlap chain and every $F_i$ has a common restriction on the same leaf set $A$, then $F_1\cup\cdots\cup F_m$ has a common restriction on $A$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on the chain length. The empty and singleton cases are immediate. For the induction step, the tail union has a common witness. Since $F_1\cap F_2$ is nonempty and $F_2$ lies in the tail union, two-family overlap gluing combines $F_1$ with that union.
\end{proof}

This is a finite local-to-global principle. Agreement witnesses are locally constant data propagated along a connected path. The overlap hypothesis is essential: disjoint coherent families may carry different witnesses.

\section{Finite information}

\begin{lemma}[Support]
If $s\in\restr{T}{A}$, then $s\subseteq A$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By definition $s=t\cap A$ for some $t\in T$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Split-Side Information Bound]
For every finite split system $T$ and finite leaf set $A$,
\[
|\restr{T}{A}|\leq 2^{|A|}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The support lemma embeds $\restr{T}{A}$ into the power set $\mathcal P(A)$, which has cardinality $2^{|A|}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Crude State-Space Bound]
On an $a$-element leaf set, there are at most
\[
2^{2^a}
\]
possible finite split systems.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
A split system is a subset of the $2^a$-element set $\mathcal P(A)$.
\end{proof}

This estimate is universal but not sharp for trees. Genuine edge splits satisfy compatibility. For $a=4$, arbitrary split systems number at most $2^{16}=65{,}536$, whereas resolved binary quartet topologies number only three. Compatibility-sensitive encodings must exploit this gap.

\section{Agreement thresholds and quartet transfer}

\begin{definition}[Agreement Threshold]
For natural numbers $N,k,n$, say that $N$ is an agreement threshold for $k$ systems and requested size $n$ if every family of $k$ split systems on a common $N$-element leaf set has an $n$-element subset on which all restrictions agree.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Size Necessity]
If $N$ is an agreement threshold for requested size $n$, then $n\leq N$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The threshold produces an $n$-element subset of an $N$-element ambient set.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[One-Tree Threshold]
For one tree on $N$ leaves, agreement on $n$ leaves is guaranteed if and only if $n\leq N$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Necessity is the size bound. If $n\leq N$, choose any $n$ leaves. A singleton family agrees with itself, with its restriction as witness.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Threshold Monotonicity]
If $N$ forces a common agreement subtree of size $n$ among every $k$-system family and $m\leq n$, then $N$ also forces one of size $m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose an $n$-element agreement set $A$, then an $m$-element subset $B\subseteq A$. Common-agreement heredity applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quartet Transfer]
Let $n\geq4$. If $N$ forces a common agreement subtree of size $n$ among every family of $k$ systems, then the same $N$ forces a common agreement subtree of size $4$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply threshold monotonicity with $m=4$. Equivalently, select any four leaves from the common $n$-leaf agreement set and invoke heredity.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Uniform Transfer]
Suppose $B(k,n)$ forces an $n$-leaf common agreement subtree for every $k$ and every $n\geq4$. Then the same $B(k,n)$ forces a common quartet for every such $k,n$.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Apply the Quartet Transfer Theorem pointwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Fourfold-Iterate Transfer]
Let $f:\mathbb N\to\mathbb N$ and $C:\mathbb N^2\to\mathbb N$, and set
\[
N(k,n)=f(f(f(f(C(k,n))))).
\]
If $N(k,n)$ forces an $n$-leaf common agreement subtree and $n\geq4$, then the same $N(k,n)$ forces a common quartet.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
Quartet transfer depends only on the threshold property and $n\geq4$, not on the formula defining the ambient number. Hence the fourfold iterate is unchanged.
\end{proof}

The corollary is a transfer principle, not an independent derivation of a fourfold iterated-exponential bound. Its value is to show that once the larger-subtree estimate is available, passage to quartets causes no additional asymptotic loss.

\section{Algorithms and examples}

A canonical restriction algorithm intersects every split side with $A$ and inserts the results into a hash set. If $r=|T|$ and $a=|A|$, its ordinary set-based running time is $O(ra)$; bit vectors give $O(r\lceil a/w\rceil)$ word operations. The output has at most $\min(r,2^a)$ sides.

For common agreement, compute the first restriction as a base and compare every other restriction with it. Correctness is the Base-Tree Characterization, and the running time for $k$ systems of at most $r$ sides is $O(kra)$ under the same model.

For chain propagation, verify each local witness and one shared index for each consecutive pair, then merge from left to right. With precomputed restrictions, work is linear in the total family-incidence count, apart from state comparisons.

As an explicit composition example, let
\[
T=\{\{1,2,3\},\{2,4\},\{1,4,5\}\},\quad
A=\{1,2,4\},\quad B=\{2,4,5\}.
\]
Then
\[
\restr{T}{A}=\{\{1,2\},\{2,4\},\{1,4\}\},
\]
and restricting again to $B$ gives
\[
\{\{2\},\{2,4\},\{4\}\}=\restr{T}{A\cap B}.
\]
For overlap gluing, take $F_1=\{1,2\}$, $F_2=\{2,3\}$, and $F_3=\{3,4\}$. Agreement within each pair on $A$ implies agreement of all four systems on $A$, although no index belongs to all three families.

For $a=0,1,2,3,4,5$, the split-side bounds are $1,2,4,8,16,32$, while the crude state counts are $2,4,16,256,65{,}536,4{,}294{,}967{,}296$. This growth motivates compatibility-sensitive compression.

\section{Applications, limitations, and future work}

The framework supports consensus phylogenetics, where several genes, bootstrap samples, or inference methods produce different trees; distributed analyses, where local teams compare overlapping tree families; Ramsey-style bounds, where a larger common subtree is first forced; and information compression, where the universal powerset estimate is a benchmark.

The abstraction has deliberate limits. Arbitrary split systems need not be compatible and need not encode binary trees. The information theorem counts subset-valued split sides, not tree topologies. Moreover, a quantitative fourfold bound must come from an additional compatible-tree argument. The structural results show only that such a bound transfers losslessly to quartets.

Four directions follow naturally. First, compatible quartet signatures may compress restricted trees far below the crude $2^{2^a}$ state count. Second, chain gluing should extend to arbitrary connected overlap graphs, using a spanning tree of the intersection graph. Third, four rounds of signature refinement may separately control leaf placement, split compatibility, tree identity, and family identity. Fourth, quartet signatures suggest constrained ternary error-correcting codes: large packings that avoid shared quartet coordinates may yield exponential lower bounds, subject to global tree consistency.

\section{Conclusion}

Restriction by intersection gives an exact algebra for agreement subtrees. It composes, distributes over union, and makes agreement hereditary. Common agreement in a nonempty family is equivalent to base-tree agreement and to pairwise agreement. Witnesses glue across overlaps and propagate along finite chains. A restriction to $a$ leaves contains at most $2^a$ sides, supplying a universal finite-state benchmark.

Agreement thresholds are bounded by ambient size, exact for one tree, and monotone in requested size. Most importantly, every threshold forcing agreement on at least four leaves forces a quartet at the same ambient size. Any fourfold iterated-exponential multiple-tree agreement bound therefore transfers without loss to the common-quartet problem. The remaining quantitative challenge is precisely the exploitation of phylogenetic compatibility.

\end{document}

